  "result": "Fixed. The command now prints `27.06`.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded the decimal part on the wrong side. It used `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")`, which turns `\"5\"` into `\"05\"`, so `12.5` was read as 1205 cents (12.05) instead of 1250. That gave 2 \u00d7 12.05 = 24.10, plus 8.25% tax = 26.09.\n\n**Fix:** I changed it to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`. Now `12.5` becomes 1250 cents, so 2500 plus 206 tax = 2706, which prints as `27.06`. I also checked other inputs, and they still parse correctly: `12` \u2192 1200, `12.34` \u2192 1234, `0.05` \u2192 5, `3.` \u2192 300.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed, so I ran them with `python3 -m unittest`, and all 6 pass. None of the existing tests covers `to_cents` with a single decimal digit, which is why this got through. I can add a small regression test for it in a `tests/test_money.py` if you'd like.",
